{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 4 — supporting detail moved out of the description (P4 admission limit)\n\nThe description exceeded the 8,000-char plan-review admission cap. Per `P4`'s own remedy —\nkeep the problem, constraints and ACs in the description; move investigation narration into\ncomments — the measurement detail lives here.\n\n### Why the \"lean\" default was not lean (the 88% figure)\n\nMeasured on this store, 2,855 tickets. Lean vs full: 44.4 MB vs 73.0 MB — a 39% saving that\nleaves the bulk untouched. Aggregate field weights of the **lean** payload:\n\n| field | bytes | share of lean |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| `authorship_ledger` | 26,241,422 | 59% |\n| `attestations` | 10,370,251 | 23% |\n| `signature` | 2,639,114 | 6% |\n| everything else | 5,141,280 | 12% |\n\nPer row: 15,548 bytes lean; 1,726 with those four (incl. `keyring`) dropped — 9x.\n\n### Why the budget had to measure the wire payload, not the reducer rows\n\nSizing raw dicts under-estimates on two axes. At 2,855 rows:\n\n| stage | bytes | delta |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| raw reducer dicts | 552,772 | — |\n| after `TicketStateOut.model_validate` | 1,526,327 | +973,555 |\n| after `js_safe_result` | 1,537,747 | +11,420 |\n\nA 178% under-estimate: the 90,000-byte budget was in truth passing ~250,000-byte payloads.\nThe dominant axis is the model's DECLARED defaults being re-added after the lean projection\ndropped them, not the 19-digit-int quoting.\n\n### `ready_tickets` headroom, and why the budget was not inflated\n\nAfter projection: 58-59 rows, 87,893-88,480 wire bytes against 90,000 — ~1.7% headroom,\n~1,499 B/row, so roughly one more ready ticket makes the default call refuse. The budget is\ndeliberately not raised: it is the client's real constraint, and raising it moves the failure\nback into the client where there is no structured signal. Instead the refusal is made useful.\n`ready_tickets` takes only `sort` and `full` — no filter arguments — so a shared \"narrow with\nhas_tag\" message would name parameters it rejects; its refusal points at `next_batch(epic_id)`.\n\nFollow-up filed: `lucky-egoistic-akitainu` (`0c99-8f2b-0de7-48f2`), linked `discovered_from`,\ncarrying the discovery-shaped-projection options (234 B/row via a new closed output model =\n~384 rows; 801 B/row via further field narrowing within `TicketStateOut` = ~112 rows).\n\n### Surfaces measured for the breaking-change note\n\nCLI `rebar list --status closed`, 2,696 rows, same ticket in each run: 39 keys -> 35 (losing\nexactly `authorship_ledger`, `attestations`, `signature`, `keyring`), 43,661,976 -> 4,747,485\nbytes, 9.2x. `--full` restores all four plus the bodies (41 keys, 71,707,799 bytes)."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787954181669811001,"uuid":"2770d763-bf0c-4bc9-a233-30fb84d2c670"}